{
  "image_name": "2f7f7a43ce1f2bcce7344eaaa2b32414.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how different immune cell populations are recruited from the blood into the tumor microenvironment and then help tumors grow. At the top, several suppressive or tumor-promoting cell types are shown with their chemokine receptors, including granulocytic and monocytic MDSCs, Treg cells, TH22 cells, IL-22–producing ILCs, and pDCs. Each is linked to matching chemokines that appear to guide recruitment into tumors, such as CXCL8, CCL2, CXCL5, CXCL12, CCL22, CCL28, and CCL20.\n\nIn the tumor environment below, these recruited cells collectively shift immunity away from antitumor activity. They inhibit key effector cells, especially CD8+ T cells and NK cells, and alter helper T-cell responses. The diagram highlights several consequences of this immune reshaping: promotion of cancer stemness, local suppression of the antitumor immune response, and stimulation of angiogenesis. These effects feed back to support the tumor cell mass.\n\nOverall, the image presents a model in which chemokine-driven trafficking of specific immune subsets into tumors creates an immunosuppressive niche that weakens tumor-killing responses and fosters tumor expansion and cancer progression. Some receptor–ligand details are small, but the central message is clear.",
